cartridge "EMPIRE/C. M. BLAKEMORE/1986"

' EMPIRE - a trading adventure
' A ColecoVision game based on "Empire" by Cleveland M. Blakemore,
' Ahoy! October 1987 (Commodore 128, BASIC 7.0).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' listing, orig/empire.bas), the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision:
' a drawn realm map with seasonal grass and moving water, a gliding
' traveller who takes a boat on the water, a gliding hand in the menus,
' sound effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: one year (four seasons of 251 steps of travel each) to buy as
' much of the realm's land as you can. Walk the map to the eight
' kingdoms; at a gate press the button to trade with its ruler. Each
' kingdom sells one speciality (and its land shares) and buys the other
' goods according to its demand. Haggle over every price. At year's end
' your land shares out of the realm's 2700 furlongs are your share of
' the empire.
'
' From the original: start with $500; the eight kingdoms, rulers,
' specialities and stocks (fabrics 400, food 250, horses 840, artifacts
' 220, weapons 325, (slaves) 500, rare metals 900, armor 475); demand 1-8
' drawn at random for the other goods, none for the speciality and the
' land; average prices and the four seasons' changes (DATA 2110-2190);
' the menus BUY, SELL, SEASONAL CHANGES, INQUIRE, INVENTORY, BID
' FAREWELL; buying: the asking price starts at P + 8 x (P/8), an offer
' within half a step (P/8) is taken, an offer within four steps brings
' the ask down to the offer plus one step, ten offers at most, the
' ruler's remarks graded by the offer; selling: the ruler takes demand x
' 12.4% of your stock, offers P - P/8, an offer within half a step is
' taken, within three steps the offer rises a step, ten tries; after a
' sale the demand falls to negligible; land cannot be bought on debt
' notes, other goods on up to $5000 of debt; land cannot be sold; time
' runs only on the map (a step each pass); year's end: inventory, cash and
' the share of the empire (land / 2700).
'
' Liberties: the realm is redrawn for 28 x 20 cells (same kinds of
' ground: grass, roads, marsh, water crossed by boat; trees, mountains and
' castle walls block), the eight kingdoms in the original's places
' roughly. A step every 8 frames (about 33 seconds a season). The buying
' ask never falls below P - 2 x (P/8): in the listing a patient haggler
' talked any price down to almost nothing (offer the ask minus four steps
' plus one, five or six times). The listing's "slaves" are SPICES. The
' joystick number entry counts up with up (+10) and right (+1), down
' with down and left, and runs faster when held. The offer round is shown
' (ROUND n OF 10). Prices are shown in whole dollars (the C-128 added
' random cents to the INQUIRE table). A title by your share at year's end.
' The original's ending fanfare is replaced by an own short fanfare.
'
' Controls: either controller. Map: the stick walks (eight ways), a side
' button at a kingdom's gate enters. Menus: up/down and a button. Numbers:
' up/down +-10, right/left +-1, a button confirms.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always
' given. Own xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

